DEEP = [
T("__repr__: the developer's view",
  "`__str__` is the friendly text for print(). `__repr__` is the unambiguous, developer-facing text, ideally looking like the code that would rebuild the object. Lists and the interactive shell show items with `__repr__`, and print() falls back to it when there's no `__str__`.",
  "class Point:\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x = x\n        self.y = y\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f'Point({self.x}, {self.y})'\n\np = Point(1, 2)\nprint(p)           # Point(1, 2): no __str__, so repr is used\nprint([p, p])      # [Point(1, 2), Point(1, 2)]",
  "print() calls `str(obj)`, which uses `__str__` if it exists and `__repr__` otherwise. Containers like lists always call `repr()` on their items.",
  "Debugging and logging: a good `__repr__` turns `<__main__.Point object at 0x7f...>` into something you can read. If you write only one, write `__repr__`."),
Q("A Cat class defines only `__str__`, returning 'Tom'. What does `print([obj])` show?", ["Something like [<__main__.Cat object at 0x...>]", "['Tom']", "[Tom]"], "Something like [<__main__.Cat object at 0x...>]",
  "Lists show their items with `__repr__`, not `__str__`. Without a `__repr__`, you get Python's default text."),
C("A readable list of ports",
  "Add a `__repr__` to Port so printing a scan result shows: [Port(22), Port(443)]",
  "class Port:\n    def __init__(self, number):\n        self.number = number\n\n    # TODO: add __repr__ returning 'Port(N)'\n\nprint([Port(22), Port(443)])",
  "class Port:\n    def __init__(self, number):\n        self.number = number\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f'Port({self.number})'\n\nprint([Port(22), Port(443)])",
  "def __repr__(self):\n    return f'Port({self.number})'",
  [("Same shape as __str__", "`__repr__(self)` takes only self and **returns** a string. Lists call it for each item.",
    "class Tag:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f'Tag({self.name!r})'\n\nprint([Tag('new')])"),
   ("Match the text exactly", "The expected text is `Port(22)`: the class name, then the number in brackets, with no spaces.",
    "n = 8080\nprint(f'Socket({n})')")]),
T("Chains of inheritance",
  "A class can inherit from a class that itself inherits. When you call a method, Python searches the object's class, then its parent, then the grandparent, and uses the first match. This search order is the **MRO** (method resolution order), which you can see with `Class.__mro__`.",
  "class Animal:\n    def move(self):\n        return 'moves'\n\n    def eat(self):\n        return 'eats'\n\nclass Bird(Animal):\n    def move(self):\n        return 'flies'\n\nclass Penguin(Bird):\n    def move(self):\n        return 'swims'\n\np = Penguin()\nprint(p.move(), p.eat())   # swims eats\nprint([c.__name__ for c in Penguin.__mro__])\n# ['Penguin', 'Bird', 'Animal', 'object']",
  "Every class ultimately inherits from `object`. The lookup walks up the chain and stops at the first class that defines the name.",
  "Frameworks build deep hierarchies (a view inherits from a base view that inherits from object), and knowing the lookup order explains which method actually runs."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["B A", "C A", "B B"], "B A",
  "C doesn't define `who`, so the lookup moves to B, which does. Nobody overrides `kind`, so it comes from A.",
  code="class A:\n    def who(self):\n        return 'A'\n\n    def kind(self):\n        return 'A'\n\nclass B(A):\n    def who(self):\n        return 'B'\n\nclass C(B):\n    pass\n\nc = C()\nprint(c.who(), c.kind())"),
T("Polymorphism: one call, many behaviors",
  "**Polymorphism** means different classes answer the same method call in their own way. Code can loop over a mix of objects and call `shape.area()` on each, without checking which class it is.",
  "class Square:\n    def __init__(self, side):\n        self.side = side\n\n    def area(self):\n        return self.side ** 2\n\nclass Triangle:\n    def __init__(self, base, height):\n        self.base, self.height = base, height\n\n    def area(self):\n        return self.base * self.height / 2\n\nfor shape in [Square(3), Triangle(4, 5)]:\n    print(shape.area())   # 9, then 10.0",
  "Python looks up `area` on each object's own class at the moment of the call, so each object runs its own version. This is often called duck typing: if it has an `area` method, it can be used as a shape.",
  "Plugins, payment methods, file formats and game characters: new kinds can be added without changing the code that uses them."),
C("Health checks that answer for themselves",
  "A monitoring loop calls `run()` on every check. Add `run` to PingCheck: return 'OK' when `latency_ms` is under 100, otherwise 'FAIL'. The loop then prints: disk: OK, then ping: FAIL",
  "class DiskCheck:\n    name = 'disk'\n\n    def __init__(self, percent_used):\n        self.percent_used = percent_used\n\n    def run(self):\n        return 'OK' if self.percent_used < 90 else 'FAIL'\n\nclass PingCheck:\n    name = 'ping'\n\n    def __init__(self, latency_ms):\n        self.latency_ms = latency_ms\n\n    # TODO: add run(self)\n\nfor check in [DiskCheck(72), PingCheck(250)]:\n    print(f'{check.name}: {check.run()}')",
  "class DiskCheck:\n    name = 'disk'\n\n    def __init__(self, percent_used):\n        self.percent_used = percent_used\n\n    def run(self):\n        return 'OK' if self.percent_used < 90 else 'FAIL'\n\nclass PingCheck:\n    name = 'ping'\n\n    def __init__(self, latency_ms):\n        self.latency_ms = latency_ms\n\n    def run(self):\n        return 'OK' if self.latency_ms < 100 else 'FAIL'\n\nfor check in [DiskCheck(72), PingCheck(250)]:\n    print(f'{check.name}: {check.run()}')",
  "def run(self):\n    return 'OK' if self.latency_ms < 100 else 'FAIL'",
  [("Same name, own rule", "The loop calls `check.run()` on each object. PingCheck just needs its own method with that exact name; DiskCheck shows the pattern.",
    "class Dot:\n    def run(self):\n        return 'OK'\n\nprint(Dot().run())"),
   ("Use the object's latency", "Compare `self.latency_ms` with 100. A conditional expression like DiskCheck's returns one of two strings in one line.",
    "ms = 40\nprint('fast' if ms < 100 else 'slow')")]),
T("Abstract base classes",
  "Sometimes a parent class exists only to define what its children must do. Inherit from `ABC` and mark methods with `@abstractmethod`: Python then refuses to create an object of any class that hasn't implemented them all.",
  "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def area(self):\n        ...\n\nclass Square(Shape):\n    def __init__(self, side):\n        self.side = side\n\n    def area(self):\n        return self.side ** 2\n\nprint(Square(3).area())   # 9\n# Shape() would raise TypeError: can't instantiate abstract class",
  "`@abstractmethod` is a decorator: it marks the method as required. The check happens when an object is created, so a missing method is caught early, not when it's first called.",
  "Defining a contract for a family of classes, such as every storage backend must have `save` and `load`."),
Q("`Shape` has an `@abstractmethod area`. `Blob(Shape)` doesn't define `area`. What happens on `Blob()`?", ["TypeError: Blob can't be instantiated", "It works, and Blob().area() returns None", "NameError"], "TypeError: Blob can't be instantiated",
  "An abstract method must be implemented before any object can be made, so the mistake shows up immediately."),
T("Composition: has-a instead of is-a",
  "Inheritance models **is-a**: a Dog is an Animal. **Composition** models **has-a**: a Car has an Engine, so the Car stores an Engine object as an attribute and calls its methods. Composition keeps classes small and lets you swap parts.",
  "class Engine:\n    def start(self):\n        return 'vroom'\n\nclass Car:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.engine = Engine()    # a car HAS an engine\n\n    def drive(self):\n        return self.engine.start() + ', off we go'\n\nprint(Car().drive())",
  "The Car hands work to the object it holds (called delegation). Nothing is inherited; the Engine is just another attribute.",
  "Most real designs mix both. A common rule of thumb is \"prefer composition\": inherit only when the child truly is a kind of the parent."),
Q("A Library keeps a collection of Book objects. Which relationship fits?", ["Composition: a library has books", "Inheritance: a library is a book", "Neither: they can't be connected"], "Composition: a library has books",
  "A library isn't a kind of book, it contains them. Store a list of Book objects on the Library."),
C("Build with composition",
  "Give Server an `__init__` that stores `Firewall()` as `self.firewall`, and make `start` enable the firewall first. It should print: Firewall on, then Server ready",
  "class Firewall:\n    def enable(self):\n        print('Firewall on')\n\nclass Server:\n    # TODO: __init__ that stores a Firewall\n\n    def start(self):\n        # TODO: enable the firewall first\n        print('Server ready')\n\nServer().start()",
  "class Firewall:\n    def enable(self):\n        print('Firewall on')\n\nclass Server:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.firewall = Firewall()\n\n    def start(self):\n        self.firewall.enable()\n        print('Server ready')\n\nServer().start()",
  "def __init__(self):\n    self.firewall = Firewall()\n\n# and in start:\nself.firewall.enable()",
  [("The part is an attribute", "In `__init__`, create the firewall and keep it: `self.firewall = Firewall()`. A server has a firewall; it isn't one.",
    "class Battery:\n    def charge(self):\n        return 'full'\n\nclass Phone:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.battery = Battery()\n\nprint(Phone().battery.charge())"),
   ("Delegate the work", "In Server's `start`, call `self.firewall.enable()` before the print. The server asks its firewall to do the job.",
    "class Light:\n    def on(self):\n        print('light on')\n\nclass Room:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.light = Light()\n\n    def enter(self):\n        self.light.on()\n\nRoom().enter()")]),
T("Sorting and membership with dunders",
  "`__lt__(self, other)` defines `<`, and once it exists `sorted()` and `min()` work on your objects. `__contains__(self, item)` defines the `in` operator.",
  "class Song:\n    def __init__(self, title, plays):\n        self.title = title\n        self.plays = plays\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        return self.plays < other.plays\n\nclass Playlist:\n    def __init__(self, songs):\n        self.songs = songs\n\n    def __contains__(self, title):\n        return any([s.title == title for s in self.songs])\n\nsongs = [Song('B', 9), Song('A', 2)]\nprint([s.title for s in sorted(songs)])   # ['A', 'B']\nprint('A' in Playlist(songs))             # True",
  "`sorted` only ever asks \"is this one less than that one?\", so `__lt__` is all it needs. `x in obj` turns into `obj.__contains__(x)`.",
  "Making your objects feel built-in: sortable tasks by priority, cards by rank, and collections that support `in`."),
C("Sort alerts by severity",
  "Add `__lt__` to Alert so `sorted(alerts)` orders by severity, lowest first. It should print: ['cert expiring', 'disk full', 'brute force']",
  "class Alert:\n    def __init__(self, name, severity):\n        self.name = name\n        self.severity = severity\n\n    # TODO: add __lt__ comparing severities\n\nalerts = [Alert('disk full', 3), Alert('brute force', 5), Alert('cert expiring', 2)]\nprint([a.name for a in sorted(alerts)])",
  "class Alert:\n    def __init__(self, name, severity):\n        self.name = name\n        self.severity = severity\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        return self.severity < other.severity\n\nalerts = [Alert('disk full', 3), Alert('brute force', 5), Alert('cert expiring', 2)]\nprint([a.name for a in sorted(alerts)])",
  "def __lt__(self, other):\n    return self.severity < other.severity",
  [("Without __lt__, sorting fails", "Python doesn't know how to compare two Alerts and raises TypeError: '<' not supported. `__lt__` answers \"is self less than other?\"",
    "class N:\n    def __init__(self, v):\n        self.v = v\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        return self.v < other.v\n\nprint(N(1) < N(2))  # True"),
   ("Compare one attribute", "Return `self.severity < other.severity`. `other` is the second Alert in each comparison.",
    "a, b = 2, 5\nprint(a < b)")]),
T("Class methods and static methods",
  "A method marked `@classmethod` receives the class (`cls`) instead of an object. It's the standard way to write extra constructors, like building an object from a string. `@staticmethod` receives neither: it's a plain helper function kept inside the class.",
  "class Date:\n    def __init__(self, year, month, day):\n        self.year, self.month, self.day = year, month, day\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_string(cls, text):\n        y, m, d = text.split('-')\n        return cls(int(y), int(m), int(d))\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_valid_month(m):\n        return 1 <= m <= 12\n\nd = Date.from_string('2024-05-01')\nprint(d.year, d.month, Date.is_valid_month(13))   # 2024 5 False",
  "`cls(...)` calls whichever class the method was called on. If a subclass calls `from_string`, it gets an object of the subclass, which a hard-coded `Date(...)` wouldn't do.",
  "Alternative constructors (`from_json`, `from_row`, `dict.fromkeys`) and small helpers that belong with a class but don't need an object."),
C("Build log entries from lines",
  "Add a classmethod `from_line(cls, line)` to LogEntry that splits a line like '12:01|ERROR|disk full' on `|` and returns `cls(time, level, message)`. It should print: ERROR disk full",
  "class LogEntry:\n    def __init__(self, time, level, message):\n        self.time, self.level, self.message = time, level, message\n\n    # TODO: @classmethod from_line\n\ne = LogEntry.from_line('12:01|ERROR|disk full')\nprint(e.level, e.message)",
  "class LogEntry:\n    def __init__(self, time, level, message):\n        self.time, self.level, self.message = time, level, message\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_line(cls, line):\n        time, level, message = line.split('|')\n        return cls(time, level, message)\n\ne = LogEntry.from_line('12:01|ERROR|disk full')\nprint(e.level, e.message)",
  "@classmethod\ndef from_line(cls, line):\n    time, level, message = line.split('|')\n    return cls(time, level, message)",
  [("The decorator goes above the def", "Write `@classmethod` on the line before `def from_line(cls, line):`. Python then passes the class as `cls`.",
    "class Box:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        self.n = n\n\n    @classmethod\n    def empty(cls):\n        return cls(0)\n\nprint(Box.empty().n)"),
   ("Split, then construct", "`line.split('|')` gives three strings. Unpack them into three names and pass them to `cls(...)`, then return the new object.",
    "a, b = 'x|y'.split('|')\nprint(b, a)")]),
Q("Why does a classmethod constructor return `cls(...)` instead of naming the class directly?", ["So a subclass calling it gets an object of the subclass", "Because the class name isn't defined yet", "cls(...) is faster"], "So a subclass calling it gets an object of the subclass",
  "`cls` is whichever class the method was called on. `Date(...)` would always build a Date, even when called as `Holiday.from_string(...)`."),
]

INTERVIEW = [
Q("Interview question: when should you use inheritance instead of composition?",
  ["When the child truly is a kind of the parent (is-a); for has-a relationships, prefer composition", "Always: inheritance is more powerful", "Never: composition is always better"],
  "When the child truly is a kind of the parent (is-a); for has-a relationships, prefer composition",
  "Inheritance couples a child tightly to its parent's details. Composition keeps parts independent and swappable."),
Q("What does `super().__init__(...)` do in a child class?", ["Calls the next class in the MRO (usually the parent) so its setup runs too", "Creates a second, parent object", "Deletes the parent's __init__"],
  "Calls the next class in the MRO (usually the parent) so its setup runs too",
  "Without it, a child that defines its own `__init__` skips the parent's, and attributes the parent sets never exist."),
Q("What's the difference between `__str__` and `__repr__`?", ["__str__ is friendly text for users; __repr__ is unambiguous text for developers, used in lists and as print's fallback", "They're identical", "__repr__ is only for numbers"],
  "__str__ is friendly text for users; __repr__ is unambiguous text for developers, used in lists and as print's fallback",
  "A good habit is to always write `__repr__`, and add `__str__` when users need a different, friendlier format."),
Q("What is polymorphism?", ["Different classes responding to the same method call in their own way", "A class having many attributes", "Converting one type into another"],
  "Different classes responding to the same method call in their own way",
  "Code calls `animal.speak()` without checking the type, and each class supplies its own `speak`."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["CBA", "C", "ABC"], "CBA",
  "C's `hi` adds 'C' and calls B's, which adds 'B' and calls A's 'A'. Each `super()` moves one step up the MRO.",
  code="class A:\n    def hi(self):\n        return 'A'\n\nclass B(A):\n    def hi(self):\n        return 'B' + super().hi()\n\nclass C(B):\n    def hi(self):\n        return 'C' + super().hi()\n\nprint(C().hi())",
  feedback={"ABC": "Each method puts its own letter first, then appends what the parent returns, so C's letter comes first."}),
C("Classic: risk scores with inheritance",
  "Device's `risk()` is 2 points per missing patch. Write InternetFacing as a child of Device whose `risk()` returns the parent's risk (use `super()`) plus 10, since it's exposed. The total should print: 20",
  "class Device:\n    def __init__(self, name, missing_patches):\n        self.name = name\n        self.missing_patches = missing_patches\n\n    def risk(self):\n        return self.missing_patches * 2\n\nclass InternetFacing(Device):\n    # TODO: risk() = parent risk + 10\n    pass\n\nfleet = [Device('laptop-7', 3), InternetFacing('vpn-gw', 2)]\nprint(sum([d.risk() for d in fleet]))",
  "class Device:\n    def __init__(self, name, missing_patches):\n        self.name = name\n        self.missing_patches = missing_patches\n\n    def risk(self):\n        return self.missing_patches * 2\n\nclass InternetFacing(Device):\n    def risk(self):\n        return super().risk() + 10\n\nfleet = [Device('laptop-7', 3), InternetFacing('vpn-gw', 2)]\nprint(sum([d.risk() for d in fleet]))",
  "def risk(self):\n    return super().risk() + 10",
  [("Extend, don't copy", "Instead of repeating the patch math, call the parent's version with `super().risk()` and add to it. If the base scoring changes, exposed devices follow.",
    "class Base:\n    def price(self):\n        return 10\n\nclass Deluxe(Base):\n    def price(self):\n        return super().price() + 5\n\nprint(Deluxe().price())  # 15"),
   ("Polymorphism does the rest", "The last line calls `risk()` on both devices. The laptop scores 3 * 2 = 6 and the VPN gateway 2 * 2 + 10 = 14, so 20.",
    "print(6 + 14)")]),
C("Classic: comparing software versions",
  "Patch levels compare as numbers, not text: '1.10.0' is newer than '1.2.9'. Give Version `__lt__`, `__eq__` and `__repr__` (like `Version('1.2.9')`), comparing tuples of ints. It should print the sorted list, then True",
  "class Version:\n    def __init__(self, text):\n        self.text = text\n        self.parts = tuple([int(p) for p in text.split('.')])\n\n    # TODO: __lt__, __eq__, __repr__\n\nprint(sorted([Version('1.10.0'), Version('1.2.9'), Version('1.2.10')]))\nprint(Version('2.0.0') == Version('2.0.0'))",
  "class Version:\n    def __init__(self, text):\n        self.text = text\n        self.parts = tuple([int(p) for p in text.split('.')])\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        return self.parts < other.parts\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return self.parts == other.parts\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Version('{self.text}')\"\n\nprint(sorted([Version('1.10.0'), Version('1.2.9'), Version('1.2.10')]))\nprint(Version('2.0.0') == Version('2.0.0'))",
  "def __lt__(self, other):\n    return self.parts < other.parts\n\ndef __eq__(self, other):\n    return self.parts == other.parts\n\ndef __repr__(self):\n    return f\"Version('{self.text}')\"",
  [("Tuples compare item by item", "`(1, 10, 0) < (1, 2, 9)` is False because 10 > 2, which is what you want. As text, '1.10.0' < '1.2.9' is True, the classic bug.",
    "print((1, 10, 0) < (1, 2, 9), '1.10.0' < '1.2.9')  # False True"),
   ("Three methods, three jobs", "`__lt__` and `__eq__` compare `self.parts` with `other.parts`. `__repr__` returns the text lists show, with the version in quotes: use double quotes around the f-string.",
    "v = '3.1'\nprint(f\"Release('{v}')\")")]),
C("Classic: a linked list",
  "Each Node holds a value and a link to the next node. Finish LinkedList's `append` (walk to the last node and link a new one) so it prints: [1, 2, 3] then 3",
  "class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nclass LinkedList:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.head = None\n\n    def append(self, value):\n        # TODO: add a Node with value at the end\n        pass\n\n    def to_list(self):\n        out, node = [], self.head\n        while node:\n            out.append(node.value)\n            node = node.next\n        return out\n\nll = LinkedList()\nfor v in [1, 2, 3]:\n    ll.append(v)\nprint(ll.to_list())\nprint(len(ll.to_list()))",
  "class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nclass LinkedList:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.head = None\n\n    def append(self, value):\n        new = Node(value)\n        if self.head is None:\n            self.head = new\n            return\n        node = self.head\n        while node.next:\n            node = node.next\n        node.next = new\n\n    def to_list(self):\n        out, node = [], self.head\n        while node:\n            out.append(node.value)\n            node = node.next\n        return out\n\nll = LinkedList()\nfor v in [1, 2, 3]:\n    ll.append(v)\nprint(ll.to_list())\nprint(len(ll.to_list()))",
  "new = Node(value)\nif self.head is None:\n    self.head = new\n    return\nnode = self.head\nwhile node.next:\n    node = node.next\nnode.next = new",
  [("Empty list first", "If `self.head` is None, the new node becomes the head and you're done.",
    "class Node:\n    def __init__(self, v):\n        self.value, self.next = v, None\n\nhead = None\nif head is None:\n    head = Node(7)\nprint(head.value)"),
   ("Otherwise walk to the end", "Start at the head and follow `.next` until a node's next is None. That's the last node: set its `.next` to the new node. This walk makes append O(n).",
    "class Node:\n    def __init__(self, v):\n        self.value, self.next = v, None\n\na, b = Node(1), Node(2)\na.next = b\nnode = a\nwhile node.next:\n    node = node.next\nprint(node.value)  # 2")]),
C("Debug: the forgotten parent setup",
  "This crashes with AttributeError: Server has no 'hostname'. Server's `__init__` replaces Device's and never runs it. Call the parent's `__init__` so it prints: web-01 linux",
  "class Device:\n    def __init__(self, hostname):\n        self.hostname = hostname\n\nclass Server(Device):\n    def __init__(self, hostname, os):\n        self.os = os\n\ns = Server('web-01', 'linux')\nprint(s.hostname, s.os)",
  "class Device:\n    def __init__(self, hostname):\n        self.hostname = hostname\n\nclass Server(Device):\n    def __init__(self, hostname, os):\n        super().__init__(hostname)\n        self.os = os\n\ns = Server('web-01', 'linux')\nprint(s.hostname, s.os)",
  "Add this as the first line of Server's __init__:\nsuper().__init__(hostname)",
  [("A child's __init__ replaces the parent's", "Once Server defines `__init__`, Python runs only that one. Device's line `self.hostname = hostname` never happens.",
    "class A:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.a = 1\n\nclass B(A):\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.b = 2\n\nprint(hasattr(B(), 'a'))  # False"),
   ("Run the parent's setup too", "Start Server's `__init__` with `super().__init__(hostname)`, then set os as before.",
    "class A:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.a = 1\n\nclass B(A):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.b = 2\n\nprint(B().a, B().b)")]),
]
